WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 329: Queen Victoria (Part 1): Accession at Eighteen, Albert the Prince Consort, the Great Exhibition of 1851, and the Workshop of the World

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:14.480
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series begins a grand two-part chronicle of the sovereign who reigned over Britain's imperial zenith.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:22.080
[Rebecca] An 18-year-old girl who inherited an unstable realm and forged the greatest empire in world history, Queen Victoria.

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.800
[Rebecca] Here is George with part 1: The Young Queen and Prince Albert.

6
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:40.320
[George] Born in May 1819 at Kensington Palace, Alexandrina Victoria was the only child of Prince Edward, Duke of Kent, and Princess Victoria of Saxe-Coburg-Salfeld.

7
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:50.560
[George] Her childhood was an oppressive ordeal governed by the Kensington system, a suffocating regime of emotional manipulation devised by her mother and Sir John Conroy.

8
00:00:50.560 --> 00:01:00.800
[George] Young Victoria was never permitted to walk downstairs without someone holding her hand, slept in her mother's bedroom every night, and was forbidden from playing with other children.

9
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.680
[George] But Victoria possessed an iron will beneath her diminutive 4'11 frame.

10
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:24.880
[George] On the morning of June 20, 1837, at 6 in the morning, 18-year-old Victoria was awakened and came downstairs in her cotton nightgown and shawl to meet the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Lord Chamberlain, who knelt on the carpet and informed her that King William IV was dead and she was queen.

11
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.400
[George] Victoria recorded in her diary with thrilling resolve.

12
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:35.280
[George] Since it has pleased Providence to place me in this station, I shall do my utmost to fulfill my duty towards my country.

13
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:47.920
[George] I am very young and perhaps in many, though not in all things, inexperienced, but I am sure that very few have more good will and more real desire to do what is fit and right than I have.

14
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.360
[George] Her very first royal act was to command that her bed be moved out of her mother's room forever.

15
00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:03.080
[George] Victoria's childhood had been an agonizing ordeal under the rigid, oppressive Kensington system, devised by her ambitious mother and Sir John Conroy.

16
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:16.200
[George] Under this controlling regime, the young princess was never permitted to walk downstairs without someone holding her hand, was forbidden from meeting other children, and slept in her mother's bedroom every single night of her life.

17
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:27.080
[George] Conroy had ruthlessly attempted to force Victoria to sign a declaration naming him her private secretary and regent, even tormenting her while she lay delirious with typhoid fever.

18
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:39.560
[George] When the Archbishop of Canterbury knelt before her at dawn on June 20, 1837, Victoria experienced not merely the awe of royal accession, but intoxicating personal emancipation.

19
00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:49.320
[George] Within hours, she exiled Sir John Conroy from her presence and moved her bed into her own private chamber, claiming absolute independence as sovereign.

20
00:02:49.320 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] In her first years on the throne, the teenage queen found a father figure, political mentor, and charming companion in her Whig prime minister, William Lamb, Viscount Melbourne.

21
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.680
[George] Handsome, worldly, and sixty years old, Lord M.

22
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:12.360
[George] spent hours daily with Victoria, tutoring her in constitutional history, government dispatch reading, and court etiquette.

23
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.400
[George] Victoria adored him with romantic devotion.

24
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:21.000
[George] But her partisan Whig loyalty sparked the bedchamber crisis of May 1839.

25
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:36.200
[George] When Melbourne resigned following a parliamentary defeat and Tory leader Sir Robert Peel was summoned to form a government, Peel politely requested that the young Queen replace a few of her Whig ladies in waiting with Tory ladies to demonstrate political neutrality.

26
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:42.280
[George] Victoria was beside herself with rage, viewing Peel as a cold, unfeeling usurper.

27
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:48.160
[George] She flatly refused to dismiss a single lady, declaring, I will not give up any of my ladies.

28
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.280
[George] Peel refused to take office under such conditions, Melbourne returned to power.

29
00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:00.400
[George] And the eighteen-year-old queen had single-handedly brought down a government, proving her fierce royal obstinacy.

30
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.160
[George] Lord Melbourne's paternal influence over the teenage Victoria was profound and all-encompassing.

31
00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:16.400
[George] They spent six hours every day together, riding through Windsor Great Park, reviewing state papers, and discussing history, literature, and European diplomacy.

32
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:25.760
[George] Melbourne treated the young Queen with chivalrous devotion, gently smoothing her impulsive temper while instilling an enduring respect for constitutional governance.

33
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:32.960
[George] When Peel demanded the replacement of her Whig ladies of the bedchamber in 1839, Victoria was indignant.

34
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.400
[George] Writing in her journal, I would not give up one of them, I never saw such conduct.

35
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:47.680
[George] Though her refusal caused a brief constitutional uproar, Peel gracefully stood down, allowing Melbourne's administration to continue for two more years.

36
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.480
[George] During which Victoria blossomed into a confident, self-assured monarch.

37
00:04:52.480 --> 00:05:00.960
[George] In October 1839, Victoria's German cousin, Prince Albert of Saxe Coburg and Gotha, arrived at Windsor Castle.

38
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:13.840
[George] The young Queen, who had previously declared she had no intention of marrying, was instantly overwhelmed by Albert's dazzling good looks, broad intellect, and exquisite courtly charm, recording in her journal.

39
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.800
[George] Albert's beauty is most striking, he is so amiable, so unaffected, in short, so fascinating.

40
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.280
[George] He has such beautiful blue eyes, an exquisite nose, and such a pretty mouth with delicate moustachios.

41
00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:28.960
[George] Because she was sovereign.

42
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.600
[George] Protocol dictated that Victoria had to propose to Albert.

43
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:39.000
[George] On October 15, summoning Albert to her private blue closet, Victoria proposed marriage.

44
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.800
[George] Albert embraced her with tears of joy, married at the Chapel Royal in St.

45
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:51.400
[George] James's Palace in February 1840, their union became the supreme royal partnership in British history.

46
00:05:51.400 --> 00:06:06.200
[George] Albert became Victoria's private secretary, intellectual compass, and indispensable governing partner, fathering nine children who would marry into every royal dynasty in Europe, earning Victoria the title, the grandmother of Europe.

47
00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:11.160
[George] Prince Albert's introduction to the British court was fraught with aristocratic prejudice.

48
00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:19.800
[George] Courtiers mocked his German accent, his serious, intellectual demeanour, and his lack of interest in drinking, gambling, or hunting.

49
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:28.120
[George] Yet within the palace walls, Albert established himself as Victoria's indispensable partner and unofficial private secretary.

50
00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:39.160
[George] He reorganized the chaotic royal finances, reformed the antiquated household administration, and brought meticulous German efficiency to the daily handling of dispatch boxes.

51
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:45.960
[George] Victoria relied on him utterly, confessing in her private letters, Albert grows daily fonder of me.

52
00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.040
[George] And I love him to adoration.

53
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.000
[George] He is my angel, my guide in all things.

54
00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:00.120
[George] Their growing family, eventually numbering four sons and five daughters, became the idyllic Victorian model of moral rectitude and domestic virtue.

55
00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:15.760
[George] Victoria's early reign coincided with the heroic age of the Industrial Revolution, when Britain was transformed by steam power, iron foundries, and the rapid expansion of the railways under engineers like George Stephenson and Isambard Kingdom Brunnell.

56
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:29.440
[George] In June 1842, Victoria became the first British monarch to travel by train, journeying from Slough to Paddington aboard the Great Western Railway at 40 miles an hour, declaring the journey delightful and rapid.

57
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.040
[George] But the era was also marked by profound social agony.

58
00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:45.920
[George] The Hungry Forties witnessed the catastrophic Irish potato famine, where over one million people starved to death and two million emigrated, and the rise of the Chartist movement demanding democratic voting rights.

59
00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:57.600
[George] In April 1848, as revolutions toppled monarchies across continental Europe, 50,000 Chartists gathered on Kennington Common to present a monster petition to Parliament.

60
00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:09.280
[George] Victoria was evacuated to Osborne House on the Isle of Wight for safety, but the British state held firm, diffusing the protest peacefully through civic resilience and industrial prosperity.

61
00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:21.040
[George] The 1840s transformed the daily reality of British life as iron rails spread like a spider's web across the countryside, connecting coalfields, factories and bustling ports.

62
00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:35.440
[George] In June 1842, Queen Victoria became the first British monarch to travel by train, journeying from Slough to Paddington on the Great Western Railway in just 25 minutes, declaring the experience charming and so quick.

63
00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:45.840
[George] But the decade was also scarred by the catastrophic Irish potato famine, which killed over a million people and forced two million more to emigrate across the Atlantic.

64
00:08:45.840 --> 00:09:01.560
[George] Victoria contributed £2,000 of her personal funds to Irish relief, while Prime Minister Sir Robert Peel courageously split his own Conservative Party to repeal the Corn Laws, ushering in the era of free trade that fed Britain's booming urban masses.

65
00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:14.600
[George] The crowning monument to Prince Albert's intellectual vision and Britain's global supremacy was the great exhibition of the works of industry of all nations, opened on May 1, 1851, in London's Hyde Park.

66
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:23.640
[George] Overcoming fierce parliamentary opposition and public skepticism, Albert championed the revolutionary architectural design of Joseph Paxton.

67
00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:34.600
[George] The Crystal Palace, a colossal greenhouse structure of prefabricated cast iron and 400 tons of plate glass, measuring 1,851 feet long.

68
00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:45.800
[George] Inside, beneath towering elm trees enclosed beneath the glass vaults, over 100,000 exhibits from across the globe displayed the technological triumphs of the human race.

69
00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:53.880
[George] Steam engines, electric telegraphs, cotton spinning mules, Indian silks, and the giant Coinor Diamond.

70
00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:59.880
[George] Six million visitors, a third of Britain's entire population, marvelled at the spectacle.

71
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:07.000
[George] Victoria attended opening day in tears of joy, recording it as the proudest, happiest day of her life.

72
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:14.440
[George] Britain stood before the world as the undisputed workshop of the world and the supreme champion of international peace and progress.

73
00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:27.160
[George] The Crystal Palace, engineered by Joseph Paxton using revolutionary cast iron framing and sheet glass, covered 19 acres of Hyde Park and enclosed living elm trees under its barrel vault.

74
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:38.440
[George] Over six million visitors flocked to the exhibition, marveling at six-ton steam hammers, electric telegraphs, printing presses, and the dazzling Coinor Diamond from India.

75
00:10:38.440 --> 00:11:00.480
[George] The exhibition generated a massive financial surplus of £186,000, which Albert astutely invested in acquiring land in South Kensington, creating the enduring cultural district nicknamed Albertopolis, home to the Victoria and Albert Museum, the Science Museum, the Natural History Museum, and the Royal Albert Hall.

76
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:07.200
[George] The great exhibition solidified Britain's standing as the preeminent economic powerhouse of the 19th century.

77
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:43.040
[George] In 1854, Britain entered its first major European conflict in forty years, the Crimean War, allying with France and the Ottoman Empire against Tsarist Russia, while British infantry displayed heroic bravery at the Alma, in Kerman, and the tragic charge of the Light Brigade at Balaklava, the campaign was severely marred by catastrophic medical and logistical incompetence, with thousands of soldiers dying of cholera, frostbite, and gangrene in the filthy military hospitals at Scutari until Florence Nightingale revolutionized battlefield nursing.

78
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:46.640
[George] Victoria followed the war with profound maternal devotion.

79
00:11:46.640 --> 00:12:08.320
[George] She visited wounded soldiers at Chatham Hospital, knitted woollen socks for troops in the trenches, and instituted the Victoria Cross in 1856, the highest military decoration for valor, cast from the bronze of captured Russian cannon, awarded equally to private soldiers and aristocratic officers alike without regard to rank or social class.

80
00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:25.200
[George] The Crimean War exposed the horrifying incompetence of the British aristocratic officer corps and the medieval state of military medicine, as thousands of soldiers died not from Russian bullets, but from cholera, dysentery, and freezing exposure in filthy field hospitals.

81
00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:37.720
[George] Spurred by William Howard Russell's searing newspaper dispatches in the Times and Florence Nightingale's revolutionary nursing reforms at Scutari, Victoria took personal responsibility for wounded veterans.

82
00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:45.320
[George] Visiting military hospitals at Chatham and weeping openly as she pinned the bronze crosses onto the coats of maimed soldiers.

83
00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:53.640
[George] The Victoria Cross, forged from Russian cannons captured at Sebastopol, marked a profound democratic revolution in British honor.

84
00:12:53.640 --> 00:13:01.640
[George] For the very first time in imperial history, supreme valor was rewarded equally regardless of aristocratic birth or military rank.

85
00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:19.800
[George] In late 1861, Prince Albert was forty-two years old, worn down by ceaseless overwork, chronic stomach ailments, and deep anxiety over his eldest son, Albert Edward, Prince of Wales, who had engaged in a scandalous affair with an actress at Currog Camp in Ireland.

86
00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:28.200
[George] In late November, despite pouring rain and severe fatigue, Albert travelled to Cambridge to confront and counsel his errant son.

87
00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:33.400
[George] Days after his return to Windsor Castle, Albert collapsed with a violent fever.

88
00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:43.560
[George] Diagnosed with typhoid fever, though modern medical historians suspect stomach cancer, Albert's health declined catastrophically over two agonizing weeks.

89
00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:52.120
[George] On the evening of Saturday, December 14, 1861, inside the blue room at Windsor Castle where George I.V.

90
00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:53.000
[George] had died.

91
00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:00.200
[George] Prince Albert breathed his last, with Victoria kneeling by his side kissing his hand, crying out in raw agony.

92
00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.600
[George] Oh, my dear darling, my Albert!

93
00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:05.880
[George] Victoria's world was extinguished in a single second.

94
00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.960
[George] She was forty two years old.

95
00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:16.640
[George] The radiant, happy Queen died that night, replaced by an inconsolable widow who would drape herself in mourning black for the next 40 years.

96
00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:26.720
[George] Prince Albert's final public service before his untimely death was an act of supreme statesmanship that prevented war between Great Britain and the United States.

97
00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:38.960
[George] In November 1861, an American Union warship intercepted the British male steamer Trent, forcibly arresting two Confederate envoys and sparking furious calls for war in London.

98
00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:52.880
[George] Suffering from racking chills and severe fever, Albert painstakingly revised the belligerent draft dispatch from the Foreign Office, softening the language to allow President Abraham Lincoln's administration to release the prisoners with honor.

99
00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:57.920
[George] Days later, Albert collapsed and died of typhoid fever at Windsor Castle.

100
00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:01.520
[George] Victoria was utterly shattered, wailing in agony.

101
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:05.840
[George] The world is gone for me.

102
00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:10.880
[George] Albert's death plunged the British crown into 40 years of impenetrable mourning.

103
00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.280
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

104
00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:34.160
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of Queen Victoria, Part 1, Excession at 18, Albert the Prince Consort, The Great Exhibition of 1851, and the Workshop of the World, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

105
00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.560
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

